{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788116177,"nonce":"WO+bsInhFZ3xDsmhsHy32A1Y7v4=","message":"AMENDMENT from spike 0.1 (FINDINGS.md, git-bug 3fa4cf5) — orchestrator, post-gate.\n\nThis spec depends on the spike more than any other; here is everything it\nbought. Scope and Acceptance stand.\n\n\nRun the advance loop in `GameComponentUpdate`, drive `DoSingleTick()` under a\nwall-clock budget with `CurTimeSpeed == Paused`, and RETURN EVERY FRAME.\nVerified against decompiled source by the orchestrator:\n\n- `Game.UpdatePlay` order is: LetterStack.OpenAutomaticLetters,\n  **TickManagerUpdate**, LetterStackUpdate, World.WorldUpdate, per-map\n  **Map.MapUpdate**, GameInfoUpdate, **GameComponentUpdate**, SignalManagerUpdate,\n  GlobalTextureAtlasManagerUpdate. So the loop's slot runs AFTER this frame's\n  MapUpdates — power nets, region/room rebuild, glow, lords, weather, areas have\n  all already run this frame.\n- `Map.MapUpdate`'s non-render half is SkyManagerUpdate,\n  powerNetManager.UpdatePowerNetsAndConnections_First, regionGrid.UpdateClean,\n  regionAndRoomUpdater.TryRebuildDirtyRegionsAndRooms, glowGrid.GlowGridUpdate_First,\n  lordManager.LordManagerUpdate, postTickVisuals, areaManager.AreaManagerUpdate,\n  weatherManager.WeatherManagerUpdate, flecks, MapComponentUpdate. The draw half\n  is gated behind `WorldRendererUtility.DrawingMap \u0026\u0026 Find.CurrentMap == this`.\n\n**Therefore: nothing per-frame needs re-implementing inside the loop.** Vanilla\nitself runs up to 300 ticks per frame against those once-per-frame managers, so\na loop that yields to the frame inherits vanilla's own correctness envelope.\nZetrith's MP does exactly this (`TickPatch.DoUpdate`: a 25ms-budget DoTick loop,\nthen return). What MP hoisted into its per-tick path\n(`AsyncTimeComp.UpdateManagers`: regionGrid/regionAndRoomUpdater/powerNetManager/\nglowGrid + pathFinder.ForceCompleteScheduledJobs) it hoisted for CROSS-CLIENT\nDETERMINISM, not correctness — single-player AutoRimmer does not need it.\nNever `while(ticks--) DoSingleTick()` unbounded.\n\n`DoSingleTick()` is public, increments `ticksGameInt` itself, and is safe to\ndrive while Paused — `TickManagerUpdate` early-returns on Paused, so there is no\ndouble-ticking. The spike ran ~950K ticks this way.\n\n\nMeasured on this machine (full v1 modlist, 3-colonist 250x250 colony; raw data\nre-derived by the orchestrator from the spike's 1026 samples):\n\n| mode | measured tps |\n|---|---|\n| Normal | 60.0 flat |\n| Fast | avg 180.0 (179-181) |\n| Superfast | avg 473.9 — 360 awake, up to 739 when `NothingHappeningInGame()` |\n| Ultrafast @60fps | avg 891.8, median 899, max 920 |\n| Ultrafast + UltraSpeedBoost | max 1913, but avg only 561.7 (a raid clamped it) |\n| budgeted loop 25ms @60fps | avg 1271.7, median 1481, range 610-1732 |\n| budgeted loop 40ms | avg 1471.3, median 1573, range 460-2108 |\n| budgeted loop 25ms @30fps | avg 1386.9, median 1392, range 500-2113 |\n\nRecommendation, to be implemented as this spec's hard cap: **max_tps default\n1000, as a per-frame `DoSingleTick` budget of at most 25ms, stopping early once\nthe second's quota is met.** At the 30 fps unwatched cap that is \u003c=75% duty —\nthe exact shape that ran sustained without incident.\n\n**Add the thermal governor here; it is cheap and it is the point.** Read\n`/sys/class/hwmon/*/temp*_input` (k10temp Tctl) off-thread once a second; while\nTctl \u003e= 93C for 30s, halve the budget; resume below 90C. Thermal reality from\n16.5 min sustained (239 samples, re-checked by the orchestrator): Tctl median\n93.4, max 94.1, **zero samples at or above 95C**, 185 of 239 at or above 93C,\nCPU fan pinned 5600-5700 rpm. The chassis plateaus at 93.5-94.1C under ANY\nsustained full-duty load — Ultrafast at 900 tps pins it exactly as hard as a\n40ms budget at 1700. There is no tps number here that is thermally free; there\nis only duty cycle. Headroom above the plateau is about 1C.\n\n\n1. **tps is state-dependent by 3x.** Per-tick cost went from ~0.5ms to ~1.3ms\n   during a raid and a heavy event window, and the same 25ms budget delivered\n   ~650 tps instead of ~1500. `max_tps` is a ceiling, never a promise — report\n   actual ticks advanced.\n2. **TimeSlower outranks the speed setting, and the budget loop is immune to\n   it.** 5s into the boosted phase a raid fired and\n   `SignalForceNormalSpeedShort` clamped `TickRateMultiplier` to 1 (60 tps) for\n   ~115s while the colony fought. The budgeted loop never consults\n   `TickRateMultiplier`, so it plowed straight through. The spike's\n   recommendation: neither silently honor nor silently ignore it — keep full\n   speed for a plain `ticks:N` advance, but stamp `{slower_active:true}` periods\n   in the result so the caller knows the storyteller considered that stretch\n   dangerous.\n3. **`TickManager.MeanTickTime` freezes while Paused** (only TickManagerUpdate\n   feeds it). A budget loop must time itself with its own stopwatch — the\n   spike's constant 0.512ms column is exactly this artifact.\n4. **`CurTimeSpeed`'s setter goes through `PlayerCanControl` and silently\n   no-ops** during cutscenes and landing confirmations. After setting a speed,\n   check `Find.TickManager.Paused` rather than assuming it took.\n\n\n`[TweakValue] private static bool UltraSpeedBoost` is written only by\n`EditWindow_TweakValues` on human interaction, so a reflection write persists\nacross frames and sessions (verified empirically, readback true for a full 180s\nphase). It is per-session state, never scribed, so no save/load cleanup.\n**But do not build `advance` on it**: it is subject to the same TimeSlower\nclamp, it is bounded by the same 45.45ms per-frame break, and the budgeted loop\nmeasured strictly better at lower fps. Resolution comment on 3fa4cf5 has the\nfull argument.\n\n\n`LetterStack.OpenAutomaticLetters()` runs once per FRAME. A burst of hundreds of\nticks in one frame can queue several letters before any opens, so\n`until:letter` must consult the LetterStack (and 1.2's journal) directly rather\nthan watch for an opened dialog, or it will halt late and miss the others.\nAlert-based watchpoints inherit a frame or two of lag for the same reason\n(alerts recompute on UIRootUpdate's cadence) — acceptable, since DESIGN already\ntreats alerts as trailing signals, but the halt tick should be honest about it.\n\nFinally: the fps cap and the tick ceiling multiply. Vanilla Ultrafast needs 60\nfps to reach its 900 (the `mult*2` per-frame ceiling); the budget loop delivers\n~1400 at 30 fps. That is one more reason this spec's loop, not vanilla speeds,\nis the advance mechanism.","files":null}]}